## Proof

We use the standard (non-strict) definition: \(x\in\mathbb{R}\) is a **local maximum** of \(f\) if there exists \(\delta>0\) such that \(f(y)\le f(x)\) for all \(y\) with \(|y-x|<\delta\).

**Claim.** A function \(f:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}\) has every point as a local maximum **if and only if** for every \(t\in\mathbb{R}\) the sub-level set
\[
\{x\in\mathbb{R}: f(x)\le t\}
\]
is open in \(\mathbb{R}\) (equivalently, every strict super-level set \(\{x:f(x)>t\}\) is closed).

---

### Necessity (\(\Rightarrow\))

Assume every point of \(f\) is a local maximum. Fix an arbitrary \(t\in\mathbb{R}\); we show \(\{f\le t\}:=\{x:f(x)\le t\}\) is open.

Let \(x_{0}\in\{f\le t\}\), so \(f(x_{0})\le t\). Since \(x_{0}\) is a local maximum, there exists \(\delta>0\) such that
\[
f(y)\le f(x_{0})\quad\text{for all }y\in(x_{0}-\delta,\,x_{0}+\delta).
\]
Combining the two inequalities gives \(f(y)\le f(x_{0})\le t\) for every \(y\in(x_{0}-\delta,x_{0}+\delta)\), hence
\[
(x_{0}-\delta,x_{0}+\delta)\subseteq\{f\le t\}.
\]
Thus every point of \(\{f\le t\}\) is an interior point, so \(\{f\le t\}\) is open. Since \(t\) was arbitrary, all sub-level sets are open. Taking complements, every strict super-level set \(\{f>t\}\) is closed.

---

### Sufficiency (\(\Leftarrow\))

Assume \(\{f\le t\}\) is open for every \(t\in\mathbb{R}\). Let \(x\in\mathbb{R}\) be arbitrary and set \(t=f(x)\). By hypothesis the set \(\{f\le f(x)\}\) is open, and it clearly contains \(x\). Therefore there exists \(\delta>0\) with
\[
(x-\delta,x+\delta)\subseteq\{f\le f(x)\},
\]
which is exactly the statement \(f(y)\le f(x)\) for all \(|y-x|<\delta\). Hence \(x\) is a local maximum. Since \(x\) was arbitrary, **every** point is a local maximum.

---

### Equivalence of the two formulations

The two statements are complementary:
\[
\{f\le t\}\text{ open }\forall t \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad \{f>t\}\text{ closed }\forall t,
\]
since \(\{f>t\}=\mathbb{R}\setminus\{f\le t\}\).

---

### Remarks on the content of the characterization

The condition is genuinely weaker than continuity, so the class is large:

- **Every constant function** satisfies it: \(\{f\le t\}\) is either \(\varnothing\) or \(\mathbb{R}\), both open.
- **Non-constant examples exist.** For instance
  \[
  f(x)=\begin{cases}1,&x=0,\\0,&x\neq 0,\end{cases}
  \]
  has every point a local maximum: at \(x=0\) all nearby values are \(\le 1\); at \(x\neq 0\) the interval \((x-|x|/2,\,x+|x|/2)\) avoids \(0\), so \(f\equiv 0\le 0=f(x)\) there. Correspondingly \(\{f>t\}\) is \(\varnothing\), \(\mathbb{R}\), or \(\{0\}\) — all closed.
- If one **additionally** assumes \(f\) is continuous, then \(\{f\le t\}\) being open for all \(t\) forces \(\{f=t\}=\{f\le t\}\setminus\bigcup_{n}\{f\le t-1/n\}\) to be both closed (continuity) and open (as the difference of an open set from a union of opens within it); on the connected space \(\mathbb{R}\) each non-empty level set is clopen, hence the whole space, so **continuous solutions are exactly the constants** — recovering the classical result.

---

### Conclusion

The functions \(f:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}\) such that every point is a local maximum are precisely those for which
\[
\boxed{\;\{x\in\mathbb{R}:f(x)\le t\}\text{ is open for every }t\in\mathbb{R}\;}
\]
(equivalently, \(\{x:f(x)>t\}\) is closed for every \(t\in\mathbb{R}\)).

